WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 72: The Cabildo and the Louisiana Purchase: The Greatest Real Estate Deal in History

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:21.200
[Rebecca] In the winter of 183, in a stately Spanish colonial municipal hall overlooking the Mississippi River in New Orleans, three men signed a piece of parchment that completely remade the geopolitical destiny of North America.

4
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:34.080
[Rebecca] President Thomas Jefferson had dispatched diplomats to Paris with a modest instruction: offer $10 million to purchase the single strategic port city of New Orleans and ensure free navigation for American flatboats.

5
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.280
[Rebecca] Instead, French Emper Napoleon Bonaparte made a stunning, impulsive counter-offer.

6
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.000
[Rebecca] For $15 million, he would sell the entire Louisiana Territory.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.400
[Rebecca] 828,000 square miles stretching from the Gulf of Mexico to the Canadian border.

8
00:00:50.400 --> 00:01:00.320
[Rebecca] George brings us the secret treaties, yellow fever rebellions, and visionary diplomacy of the Louisiana Purchase.

9
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:20.480
[George] But for the 300,000 pioneer farmers who had settled in Kentucky, Tennessee, and the Ohio Territory, the river was not merely a geographic boundary.

10
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.640
[George] It was their economic lifeline.

11
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:41.200
[George] With the Appalachian Mountains blocking trade with eastern ports like Philadelphia and New York, frontier farmers floated every barrel of pork, flour, and whiskey down the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers to the international port of New Orleans, where ocean-going cargo ships carried goods across the globe.

12
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:54.320
[George] Since 1762, the vast territory west of the river had been owned by the declining Spanish Empire, which permitted American merchants to deposit and transfer goods in New Orleans tax-free under Pinckney's Treaty.

13
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] But in October 1800, a terrifying geopolitical earthquake rocked Washington.

14
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:16.440
[George] Rumors confirmed that Spain's King Charles IV had signed the Secret Treaty of San Ildefonso, retroceding the entire Louisiana Territory back to France, now ruled by the supreme military conqueror of Europe, First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte.

15
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:32.520
[George] Western frontier farmers living along the Ohio and Cumberland Rivers depended entirely upon free navigation of the Mississippi to float their wheat, flour, and salt pork to oceangoing ships in New Orleans, threatening secession if their river outlet was closed.

16
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:48.680
[George] President Thomas Jefferson recognized that whoever controlled the mouth of the Mississippi River held a stranglehold over the economic lifeblood of the young American Republic, declaring that New Orleans was the single spot on the globe whose possessor was our natural and habitual enemy.

17
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:58.920
[George] Napoleon's grand strategic vision was not centered on the North American mainland, it was centered on the Caribbean island colony of Saint Domingue, modern-day Haiti.

18
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:11.800
[George] Saint Domingue was the wealthiest colony on earth, producing two-fifths of the world's sugar and half its coffee, built entirely on the brutal enslavement of 500,000 African men, women, and children.

19
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:23.400
[George] In 1791, enslaved workers launched the Haitian Revolution, led by the military and political genius Tausant Louverture, establishing an autonomous black republic.

20
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:33.080
[George] Napoleon envisioned the Louisiana Territory as a massive breadbasket and agricultural storehouse to feed the sugar plantations of a reconquered Saint Domingue.

21
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:47.360
[George] In December 1801, Napoleon dispatched a colossal military armada of 40,000 seasoned French troops, commanded by his brother-in-law General Charles Leclerc, to crush Louverture's forces and reestablish slavery.

22
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.880
[George] But Leclerc's army marched into a tropical catastrophe.

23
00:03:51.040 --> 00:04:00.480
[George] The Haitian freedom fighters fought with ferocious guerrilla tenacity, and within months, yellow fever, the black vomit, decimated the French ranks.

24
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:07.600
[George] Over 30,000 elite French soldiers died in agonizing delirium, including General Leclerc himself.

25
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.720
[George] Without Saint Domingue as an anchor, Napoleon's entire Western Hemispheric Empire collapsed in the Caribbean mud.

26
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:32.160
[George] The heroic resistance of Haitian freedom fighters, combined with the catastrophic ravages of yellow fever, decimated Napoleon's elite expeditionary force of 30,000 veteran soldiers, shattering his dream of an American agricultural empire to supply French sugar islands.

27
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:46.080
[George] Tausant Louverture's brilliant military strategy and the fierce determination of Saint Domingue former slaves dealt a fatal blow to Napoleon colonial ambitions, permanently altering the geopolitical destiny of North America.

28
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.560
[George] Meanwhile in Washington, President Thomas Jefferson understood the mortal peril facing the young republic.

29
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:59.280
[George] If Napoleonic France controlled New Orleans, the United States would be choked at the mouth of the Mississippi.

30
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:07.280
[George] Jefferson famously wrote There is on the globe one single spot, the possessor of which is our natural and habitual enemy.

31
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.280
[George] It is New Orleans, through which the produce of three eighths of our territory must pass to market.

32
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:24.400
[George] In January 1803, Jefferson appointed his trusted friend and future president, James Monroe, as special minister plenipotentiary to join resident ambassador Robert R.

33
00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.400
[George] Livingston in Paris.

34
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:33.480
[George] Jefferson authorized the diplomats to spend up to $10 million to purchase New Orleans and a strip of West Florida.

35
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:40.600
[George] But when Monroe arrived in Paris in April 1803, the diplomatic chessboard had shifted with blinding speed.

36
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:54.360
[George] Facing renewed war with Great Britain and needing massive cash reserves to finance an invasion of England, Napoleon suddenly decided to liquidate his entire North American portfolio before the British Royal Navy could capture it.

37
00:05:54.360 --> 00:06:09.480
[George] Livingston and Monroe had been authorized by President Jefferson to spend up to $10 million solely for the island of New Orleans and coastal West Florida, leaving them completely astonished when French diplomats offered the entire western half of the continent.

38
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:22.840
[George] Livingston and Monroe conducted delicate late-night negotiations in drafty Paris salons, weighing the immense geopolitical stakes against the lack of formal authorization from Washington to purchase an entire empire.

39
00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:31.400
[George] On April 11, 1803, French Treasury Minister François Barbé-Marbois summoned Robert Livingston for a private conference.

40
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:37.000
[George] To Livingston's utter astonishment, Barbé-Marbois did not negotiate for New Orleans.

41
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.400
[George] Instead, he asked, What will you give us for the whole of Louisiana?

42
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.880
[George] Livingston was stunned into silence.

43
00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:51.320
[George] Monroe and Livingston had neither constitutional authority nor presidential instructions to purchase an entire subcontinent.

44
00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:55.880
[George] Yet they recognized that opportunity knocks but once in the history of nations.

45
00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:04.040
[George] If they sent letters back to Washington by sailing ship for authorization, months would pass and Napoleon might change his volatile mind.

46
00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:07.640
[George] Over two weeks of intense, high-stakes negotiations.

47
00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.440
[George] The diplomats struck a breathtaking agreement.

48
00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:23.360
[George] The United States would pay France 60 million francs, $11,250,000 in United States bonds, and assume 20 million francs in debts owed by France to American citizens.

49
00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:38.480
[George] For a total purchase price of $15 million, for roughly three cents an acre, the United States acquired 828,000 square miles, an imperial domain encompassing all or part of 15 future American states.

50
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:54.160
[George] Barbé-Marbois argued that selling the vast territory to the United States would keep it out of British naval hands while providing $15 million in hard specie to finance Napoleon's upcoming military campaigns against Great Britain and European coalitions.

51
00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:08.080
[George] French finance minister Barb Marbois was intimately familiar with American finances, having served as a diplomat in Philadelphia during the Revolutionary War, and skillfully structured the purchase treaty to ensure prompt payment.

52
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:18.240
[George] When the treaty arrived in Washington in July 1803, Thomas Jefferson was thrilled by the geopolitical triumph, but tormented by intellectual conscience.

53
00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:29.760
[George] Jefferson was a strict constructionist who had spent his entire political career arguing that the federal government possessed only those powers explicitly enumerated in the United States Constitution.

54
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:38.240
[George] Nowhere did the Constitution authorize the President or Congress to purchase foreign territory or incorporate foreign populations into the Union.

55
00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.680
[George] Jefferson privately drafted a proposed constitutional amendment to legalize the purchase.

56
00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:51.440
[George] But his Secretary of State, James Madison, and Treasury Secretary Albert Gallatin urged him to abandon the amendment.

57
00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:59.520
[George] Napoleon was already showing signs of sellers' remorse, and Foreign Minister Tollirand was urging the First Consul to cancel the agreement.

58
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:15.240
[George] Setting aside his strict philosophical scruples for the supreme national interest, Jefferson submitted the treaty directly to the United States Senate, which ratified the purchase on October 20, 1803, by a decisive vote of 24-7.

59
00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:22.760
[George] Jefferson was a strict constitutionalist who believed the federal government possessed only explicitly enumerated powers.

60
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:32.360
[George] Wrestling privately with whether he had the legal right to purchase foreign territory before deciding that continental destiny superseded constitutional literalism.

61
00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:45.720
[George] Jefferson Cabinet debated whether a constitutional amendment was required, but the risk that Napoleon might change his volatile mind convinced the administration to submit the treaty directly to the Senate for immediate ratification.

62
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:53.800
[George] The transfer of power culminated on the cool morning of Tuesday, December 20, 1803, in New Orleans.

63
00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:09.240
[George] Inside the stately Sala Capitular on the second floor of the Cabildo, the Spanish Colonial Municipal Palace overlooking the Place d'armes, now Jackson Square, French colonial prefect Pierre Clément de La Set met with American Commissioners William C.C.

64
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.440
[George] Claiborne and General James Wilkinson.

65
00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:24.040
[George] Before a solemn gathering of French creoles, Spanish officials and American merchants, Laussat formally surrendered the keys of the city and released the inhabitants from their allegiance to France.

66
00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:29.080
[George] The three men stepped onto the iron-railed balcony overlooking the crowded plaza.

67
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:40.920
[George] As French military bands played, the tricolor flag of the French Republic was slowly lowered down the center flagpole, while the 15-star, 15-stripe American banner was hoisted upward.

68
00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:54.800
[George] Midway up the mast, the two flags paused for several minutes, fluttering side by side in the river breeze as cannon roared from the artillery batteries along the levee, signaling that Louisiana was now part of the American Republic.

69
00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:04.960
[George] The historic Sala Capitular inside the Cabildo had witnessed Spanish, French, and now American sovereignty in the span of three weeks, as Governor William C.C.

70
00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:13.040
[George] Claiborne and General James Wilkinson formally accepted the keys of the territory amidst artillery salutes in Jackson Square.

71
00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:25.360
[George] The tricolor flag of the French Republic was slowly lowered in Jackson Square as the American stars and stripes rose in the afternoon breeze, accompanied by the booming roar of celebratory naval cannon.

72
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:32.320
[George] The Louisiana Purchase remains the greatest diplomatic triumph and real estate acquisition in human history.

73
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:47.760
[George] With the stroke of three pens, the young American Republic doubled its geographic territory overnight, securing absolute control over the Mississippi River basin and laying the foundation for an agricultural, industrial, and continental superpower.

74
00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.600
[George] Within months, President Jefferson dispatched Captains Meriwether Lewis and William Clark from St.

75
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:59.440
[George] Louis on their epic core of discovery to explore the newly acquired wilderness to the Pacific Ocean.

76
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:03.120
[George] Today, the Cabildo still stands proudly beside St.

77
00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:09.440
[George] Louis Cathedral in the heart of New Orleans, preserved as a premier museum of American history.

78
00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:18.080
[George] Visitors can walk into the very sala where the parchment was signed, gazing out through the balcony arches at the mighty Mississippi River flowing past.

79
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:26.240
[George] An eternal monument to the visionary diplomacy that transformed a fragile string of Atlantic states into a continental empire of liberty.

80
00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:42.760
[George] The purchase of 828,000 square miles for less than three cents an acre doubled the size of the United States overnight, opening vast fertile plains and mountain ranges that would define the industrial might and democratic character of the American Republic.

81
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:56.520
[George] The vast Western Empire acquired in 1803 provided the agricultural abundance, mineral wealth, and geographic scale that transformed the United States from a coastal confederation into a global superpower.

82
00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:16.040
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey into the vibrant, mystical heart of 19th century, New Orleans for the legend of the Voodoo Queen.

